.TH BEERSSH-SESSION 1 "" "beerssh-session 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
beerssh-session \- keep a shell on a server when the connection drops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B beerssh\-session version
.br
.B beerssh\-session attach
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
beerssh-session is not run by hand. BeerSSH runs it over SSH for a host whose
.I Keep the shell on the server
option is on: the shell then belongs to this program and not to the connection,
so it goes on running through a dropped link, and whatever it printed meanwhile
is there when BeerSSH comes back. Closing the tab ends the shell.
.PP
Nothing needs configuring and no daemon runs. The program only has to be on the
PATH that sshd gives a command, for the user who logs in. It is linked statically,
so on another Linux of the same architecture copying the file there is enough.
.PP
A session keeps the last 8 MiB of output. A client away for longer than that is told
how many lines are gone. After the program ends, the session stays for 10 minutes for
a client to collect the end of it.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B version
Prints the protocol it speaks, and exits. This is what BeerSSH asks first.
.TP
.BI attach " NAME"
Joins the session called
.IR NAME ,
starting it if there is none, and speaks the session protocol on standard input
and output.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/beerssh-session/
The sockets of the sessions running for this user, or
.I /tmp/beerssh-session-UID/
where that variable is not set.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR beerssh (1),
.BR ssh (1)
